<h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Tomatoes Grow Quickly. – Lesson # 3</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 20px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Tomatoes Grow Quickly.</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Explain how to perform a simple task. interactive: Engage in questions and answers about familiar subjects.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and perform simple gardening tasks (e.g., 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.'). Can ask and answer questions about gardening when collaborating on a project (e.g., 'How do you plant the tomatoes?' 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.'). Can ask and answer simple questions about their preferences related to gardening (e.g., 'Do you like watering the plants?' 'Yes, I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging.').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of gardening vocabulary (e.g., 'seed,' 'soil,' 'watering can,' 'plant,' 'tomato,' 'dig,' 'water') introduced in previous lessons. Students quickly shout out the words. The teacher asks quick questions like, "What is this?" "Do you like plants?" "What color is this tomato?" to activate speaking and recall. Students respond with one or two words or short sentences.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models key sentences for describing tasks and preferences. "First, I dig a hole." "Then, I plant the seed." "I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging." The teacher explicitly models the /t/ sound in 'tomatoes,' 'plant,' 'water,' and 'tall,' and the /g/ sound in 'grow,' 'garden,' 'dig,' and 'green.' The teacher also models question forms: "How do you plant a seed?" "Do you like planting?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals (pictures of a garden) and gestures to clarify meaning.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1 (Meaning): "If I say 'First, I dig a hole,' what do I do first? Do I water the plant first or make a hole first?" (Points to picture of digging vs. watering)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2 (Pronunciation): "When I say 'tomato,' do my teeth touch my tongue for the /t/ sound, or is it a soft sound?" (Models /t/ sound). "Is 'grow' like 'go' or 'row'?" (Models /g/ sound).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 3 (Grammar): "If I like plants, do I say 'I like plants' or 'I not like plants'?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or printed, showing two friends, Leo and Mia, planting a tomato plant. The dialogue uses sequence words, present simple, demonstratives, preferences, and target vocabulary. Example dialogue:
          <br>Leo: "Mia, how do you plant these tomatoes?"
          <br>Mia: "First, you dig a small hole in the soil. Look, this is a seed."
          <br>Leo: "Oh, okay. Then what?"
          <br>Mia: "Second, you put the seed in the hole and cover it with soil. Then you water the plant."
          <br>Leo: "I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging holes. How often do you water them?"
          <br>Mia: "I water them every day. These tomatoes grow quickly!"
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving simple instructions related to everyday activities. Expressing simple likes and dislikes. Asking and answering questions about familiar subjects.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Oral):</b> "Who are in the dialogue?" "What are Leo and Mia doing?" "Are they in the garden or the house?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Oral):</b> The teacher reads statements, and students say "True" or "False."
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia plants flowers." (False)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo likes digging holes." (False)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They water the plants every day." (True)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"These tomatoes grow slowly." (False)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Describing gardening steps using sequence words ('first,' 'second'), present simple tense (including + 's' for third person singular), demonstratives ('this,' 'these'), question forms ('How often do you/does s/he...?', 'Do you like...?'), expressing preferences ('like,' 'don't like'), and using conjunctions ('and,' 'but'). Focused pronunciation on /t/ and /g/ sounds, and spelling 'water,' 'sun,' 'soil' orally.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Choral & Individual Oral Practice):</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences related to gardening steps with picture prompts. Students complete them orally, first as a class, then individually.
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "First, you ______ a hole." (Picture of digging) -> Students: "dig"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Then, you ______ the seed." (Picture of planting) -> Students: "plant"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "You ______ the plants every day." (Picture of watering) -> Students: "water"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "This is a ______ plant, and these are ______ tomatoes." (Pictures of a plant and tomatoes) -> Students: "tomato, green"</li>
              </ul>
              Teacher provides immediate error correction focusing on pronunciation (/t/, /g/) and grammar (e.g., "She waters").
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Q&A Drill with Preferences (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence starters or cues. Student A asks, Student B answers, then they switch roles.
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Cue: "water plants" -> Student A: "Do you like watering the plants?" Student B: "Yes, I like watering the plants." OR "No, I don't like watering the plants."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Cue: "dig holes" -> Student A: "Do you like digging holes?" Student B: "No, I don't like digging, but I like planting."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Cue: "Maria / water / every day" -> Student A: "How often does Maria water the plants?" Student B: "Maria waters the plants every day."</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Picture Sequencing and Description (Small Group/Pair Work):</b> Students receive sets of three picture cards illustrating simple gardening steps (e.g., 1. digging a hole, 2. planting a seed, 3. watering a plant). In small groups or pairs, they put the pictures in order and orally describe the steps using 'First,' 'Second,' and 'Then.' They also identify items using demonstratives and practice spelling key words.
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, you dig a hole. This is soil."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Second, you plant the seed. What letters are in 'seed'?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Then, you water the plant. How do you spell 'water'?"</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Garden Helper Role-play." Students work in pairs. One student acts as a "New Gardener" who wants to plant tomatoes, and the other is an "Experienced Gardener" who knows how to do it. The New Gardener asks questions about planting and preferences, and the Experienced Gardener explains the steps and shares their likes/dislikes.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each pair a simple picture showing gardening tools and a few steps for planting a tomato seed.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>New Gardener's prompts:</b> "How do you plant tomatoes?" "What is this?" (pointing to a seed/soil/watering can). "Do you like [digging/watering/planting]?" "How often do you water the plants?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Experienced Gardener's response requirements:</b> Explain steps using 'First,' 'Second,' 'and,' 'then.' Use demonstratives ('This is a seed,' 'These are plants'). Express preferences using 'I like...' or 'I don't like..., but I like...'. Use present simple ('I water them every day,' 'The tomatoes grow quickly.').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice their role-play for 3-4 minutes. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency and accuracy.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Selected pairs perform their role-play for the class.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Garden Helper Role-play" (Stage 4). The teacher monitors for participation, use of target language, and intelligibility.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' oral performance during the "Garden Helper Role-play."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear production of /t/ and /g/ sounds.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates words like 'garden,' 'plant,' 'tomato,' 'seed,' 'soil,' 'watering can,' 'dig,' 'water,' 'grow,' 'every day,' 'first,' 'second.'</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Explains steps in a logical sequence.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Uses present simple, demonstratives, and question forms correctly (e.g., 'How do you...?', 'Do you like...?', 'She waters...').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic functions:</b> Successfully describes steps, routines, and expresses preferences ('like,' 'don't like'). Uses conjunctions ('and,' 'but').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Engages with partner, asks and answers questions appropriately.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you say well today when talking about gardening?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing that was difficult to say, or that you want to practice more?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which part of explaining how to plant a tomato was easiest for you to say?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did you use today in your conversations?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking focus for this lesson was well-received by students, especially the role-play. The use of picture cards during practice and production significantly scaffolded their oral output. Students were able to use 'First, you...,' 'Second, you...,' and express preferences with 'like/don't like.' Some struggled with the 's' in present simple for third person singular ('Maria *waters*'). I need to integrate more targeted practice for this grammar point in future speaking activities. The pronunciation of /t/ and /g/ improved with focused modeling.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will build on their ability to orally describe gardening steps and routines by writing short paragraphs or step-by-step instructions. They will apply the vocabulary, sentence structures, and sequence words practiced today into written form.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>